🚀 What Your Private Agent Chat App Must Have

🧱 1. Multi‑Channel Replacement Layer
OpenClaw and Hermes both rely on Telegram/Slack/Discord as the “front door” for agents. Your app must replace that entire layer.

Core requirements
- Unified inbox for all agents (OpenClaw’s biggest selling point)   
- Multiple conversation types:  
  - 1:1 with an agent  
  - Group chats with multiple agents  
  - Human ↔ agent ↔ human routing  
- Real‑time streaming messages (LLM tokens, logs, tool calls)

---

🔐 2. Security Model That Fixes OpenClaw’s Problems
OpenClaw has documented security issues: plaintext credentials, supply‑chain attacks, malicious skills, and exfiltration incidents. 

Your app must include:

Security essentials
- End‑to‑end encryption (Telegram-style but actually private)  
- Local-first data storage (OpenClaw’s core philosophy)   
- Device pairing (OpenClaw uses this; you should too)   
- Skill permission sandboxing  
- Audit logs for agent actions  
- Zero telemetry mode (Hermes Agent emphasizes this) 

---

🧩 3. Agent Routing & Orchestration Layer
Your app becomes the “router” instead of Telegram/Slack.

Required capabilities
- Multi-agent routing (OpenClaw supports this)   
- Channel → Agent mapping  
- Agent switching inside a chat  
- Agent tagging (e.g., @benny, @researcher, @devops)  
- Hermes-style structured task execution logs 

---

🧠 4. Memory & Context Layer
Hermes Agent’s advantage is structured memory with FTS5 search and summarization. 

Your app needs:

- Long-term conversation memory  
- Searchable memory index  
- Per-agent memory isolation  
- Team-shared memory spaces  
- Context injection into agents

---

🛠️ 5. Skill / Tool Execution Console
OpenClaw has 52+ built-in skills and a marketplace. Hermes has 40+ tools. 

Your app must expose:

- Skill invocation UI  
- Tool call logs  
- File uploads / downloads  
- Sandboxed execution results  
- Error traces  
- Cron / automation scheduling (OpenClaw supports cron) 

---

🖥️ 6. Desktop + Mobile Native Apps
Because founders want agents where they work, and OpenClaw’s whole pitch is “your chat apps.” 

Must support:
- iOS  
- Android  
- macOS  
- Windows  
- Web fallback

---

🧬 7. Agent Identity System
You’re building Bitcoin Benny and the whole hip-hop squad — your app needs to treat agents like characters.

Identity features
- Avatar system  
- Nameplates (Fast Blaze style)  
- Personality profiles  
- Wallet permissions (for your money-enabled sandbox)  
- Agent stats dashboard (tasks completed, trades simulated, etc.)

---

💸 8. Wallet & Transaction Sandbox
Your agent sandbox uses real-money wallets for hypothetical trades. 

Your app needs:

- Wallet integration layer  
- Transaction simulation mode  
- Risk guardrails  
- Spending limits per agent  
- Audit trail for every financial action

---

🧪 9. Developer Mode (for you + your ecosystem)
Because OpenClaw is CLI-first and Hermes is SDK-first.

Dev features
- Live logs  
- Skill hot-reload  
- Agent config editor  
- Webhook tester  
- Local LLM endpoint tester  
- Model switching UI (OpenClaw supports BYOK for Claude/GPT/Gemini/xAI/Groq/Mistral) 

---

🏢 10. Team & Enterprise Features
Slack-level collaboration but agent-native.

Must include:
- Role-based access control (OpenClaw lacks this; alternatives emphasize it)   
- Team spaces  
- Shared agents  
- Shared memory vaults  
- Admin console  
- Compliance mode

---

📦 11. Plugin / Extension Marketplace
OpenClaw has ClawHub; Hermes has typed tools.

Your app needs:

- Skill marketplace  
- Versioning  
- Ratings  
- Dependency scanning (to prevent malicious skills)  
- One-click install

---

🔄 12. Automation Engine
OpenClaw has cron + webhook triggers. Hermes has natural-language cron. 

Your app should support:

- Scheduled tasks  
- Event triggers  
- Multi-step workflows  
- Parallel subagents  
- Retry logic

